al citizen a book to  study American history in American  culture and then they give you a quiz  and if you pass the quiz you could get  your so-called second papers the  declaration of intent was the first  papers five years later you judge second  papers now I was helping her and some of  those pieces of information in the  Bookroom booklet were very specific and  I asked her the standard questions of  the back and I came to the one who was  the president of the Confederacy pretty  tough question but she knew she said  jefferson davis and i asked the next  question which was who was the president  of the union and she said david Dubinsky  who else well so i was going to go to  hopkins if I could somehow get the money  together although it seemed impossible  and that friday night i was on the phone  call on the phone with my friend  leibowitz we'd only gotten a phone a  couple of years before otherwise we used  to get down the corner drugstore and  stick a nickel and I'm sure some of you  did the same and I'm talking to  Leibovitz on the phone and he says did  you apply to Yale I looked down my list  I couldn't remember where I'd applied  yeah yeah yeah I applied to Yale but did  you know they don't have exams exams it  was friday evening and monday morning  you could do this kind of thing in those  days i called the yale admissions office  and I said lying through my teeth I said  you know I was accepted to obvious but I  really want to go to Yale yeah that's  the place I want to go can I have an  interview well things were blacks are  then they said sure show up on Wednesday  be here at eleven or something like this  or it noon so I got on the train what is  now called the metro-north yield New  York New Haven and Hartford railroad i  plunked down four dollars and  thirty-five cents for a round-trip  ticket I got off in New Haven and I  walked  to the medical school and I had two  interviews and at the end of the second  interview they invited me to tea tea  imagine such a thing t he walked me down  this long corridor and that this room is  now gone it was a faculty lounge a  beautifully carpeted faculty lounge and  faculty wise every afternoon at four  o'clock serve tea and there were these  handsome young men with white buck shoes  and he's beautiful young women from the  Yale nursing school and the atmosphere  was so subdued and and so quiet and  genteel and I thought huh I don't belong  in a place like this which is exactly  why I want to go here so week later I  got my acceptance and I lived happily  ever after at least in the reference to  the yale medical school I lived happily  ever after but it was the tea and the no  exams that got me here otherwise I'd be  giving this talk Baltimore something  somewhere so I tell the story of my  father's last illness and I tell the  story of the residency program at the  Yale New Haven Hospital in those days  where there was a pyramid they chose 13  of us the first year as interns and  every year they fired a few or worse yet  they made them go into gynecology  orthopedics and bit by bit they would  filter us out and in the wood to who  ended up at the end being the chief  residents in what was called general and  cardiovascular surgery and lo and behold  on my birthday which was December eighth  nineteen fifty-eight my 28th birthday I  was notified that i was one of the two  people who had been chosen chief  resident my father was in the hospital  and I waited till Wednesday when I had  the evening off and I had a car by that  I had had a fellowship a research  fellowship were three thousand dollars  so i took 1200 of it and bought a  second-hand car and I'd had that for  three years at that point I drove down  New York and I visited my father  manav here and I told him that this  culmination of everything that we had  wished for and everything that we had  worked for had come true I was going to  be chief resident in this very waspy  place that he had never heard of ten  years earlier as a matter of fact well  he everybody heard of Harvard yeah we  knew that Harvard was a place where  Roosevelt's sons had gone to school and  we knew that Harvard and Yale had a crew  race on the Thames River in New London  every year because my grandmother used  to have a farm near there when she was  younger and that's what we knew about  Harvard and Yale until I went there to  medical school that's how completely out  of phase we were with the surrounding  culture and hair I was going to be chief  resident and it was it was a wonderful  moment for the two of us it was a  Wednesday and I went back to the  hospital was on duty friday evening and  got a phone call from the urology  resident he was on the urology service  because one of the complications of his  illness was urological and he told me  they'd found him dead in bed just two  days after I had been able to tell him  this so I tell that story as though it  resolves something as though are coming  together at that time had somehow been  accompanied while he lived and after his  death I have been consumed with a futile  effort of trying to rid myself of meijer  noodleman incidentally I tell the story  of the name change which still  fascinates me some of my cousins had  invented this name really invented it  out of whole cloth new land this was the  new country and we adopted it when I was  a senior in high school and I tell all  of the complex reasons including I have  to admit as I do here shame that I  decided to change that name to what our  cousins had done so let me start again  on this afterward for too many years  while he lived in  after his death I've been consumed with  a futile effort of trying to rid myself  of meijer noodleman he wanted to change  his name to but that would have defeated  one of our main purposes in changing  hours which was to separate ourselves  from him and what to us in our  adolescent thinking he represented still  during much of the attempted escape I've  sought to come to terms with my father  even stumbling and falling along the way  weakened and made frail my shoulders  stooped by an illness whose emotional  torment succeeded in mimicking his  physical ones I have seemed in those  dreadful hours to become him at the  worst of times fearful thoughts have had  to be burned out of my brain with great  jolts of electric current I required  much help I will never know the cost of  being Myers son men when midway in life  circuitous journey I found myself  entrapped within those dark and tangled  woods I should not have blamed him for  my loss of direction my father was  without a compass and he too had missed  his path the choices I had made and the  directions i had taken to lead me into  the morass were mine alone the  blundering attempts to loose myself from  the thickets were also mine but unlike  him I did at last find my way out even  my bad choices were not enough to  prevent my ultimate choice which was to  resume the voyage and naked in peace as  child in the synagogue on Yom Kippur I  would stand alongside my father in  tremulous awe as we in tone the ancient  formula before the heavenly decision is  made as to who shall be at peace and who  shall be tormented but penitence prayer  and good deeds can avert the severity of  decree the Hebrew word translated in the  prayer book as penitence is chuva whose  literal meaning is returned I have owed  Myra noodleman recompense for the  ravages inflicted on his years and font  from my inability to perceive his  unhappiness  it is by returning to memory and to my  father that I have sought to comprehend  my own severe decree against myself the  guilt and the sickness whose ravages are  not to be forgotten my quest has been to  perceive what he really was  understanding has come but slowly it has  taken me overly long to reach the stage  of the journey when I'm finally able to  look back and see so much of him with  full awareness I have sought to be what  he could not be but I have not been able  to deafen myself through the years to  the siren call of its opposite dark and  feeling seduction luring me to make  myself what he was and it has sometimes  unmanned me and seeking to escape him I  have drawn closer and now at last I know  that the closeness can be good I've been  trying to find his way in America for  him and for me there is no end to it and  that's the end of the book and that's  the end of I wanted what I wanted to  talk to you about this afternoon and I'm  up for grabs if anybody wants to come in  or ask any questions here we are  dr. sherwin newland his author of lost  in America it's published by Knopf a  division of Random House visit Random  House calm / Knopf for more information
 

